The first plot shows the excitatory Gaussian in orange, the inhibitory one in blue, drawn negative, and their difference in black. The second plot is the magnitude of the kernel’s Fourier transform, with the orange dashed line at its peak. With the default settings the peak sits at about 0.15 cycles per unit length, a wavelength of six to seven units, a few times the width of the inhibitory surround.
A few things are worth trying before moving on. Make inhibition wider and the peak moves to lower frequencies: the stripes the network will produce get broader. Make excitation wider, closer to the inhibition width, and the peak gets lower and broader, since the two Gaussians cancel over a larger range of frequencies. Set the inhibition strength to zero and the peak moves to frequency zero, meaning the network would simply amplify uniform activity rather than any pattern. The Mexican hat shape, not just excitation, is what makes stripes.

The nonlinearity
The function \(f\) is the neuron’s input-output relation, and it is the one ingredient the linear analysis above says nothing about. The eigenvalues of \(W\) tell you which pattern grows, but not where it stops. That is the job of \(f\), and different choices give qualitatively different networks. The selector below switches \(f\) in the simulation, and the small plot shows the function you have chosen.


Code
viewof rnNL = Inputs.radio(new Map([
  ["linear, f(x) = x", "linear"],
  ["rectified linear, f(x) = [x]₊", "relu"],
  ["rectified square, f(x) = [x]₊²", "relu2"],
  ["clipped linear, f(x) = [x]₊ cut off at 1", "clip"],
  ["sigmoid, f(x) = 1 / (1 + exp(−4(x − 1)))", "sigmoid"]
]), {value: "relu", label: "Nonlinearity f"})
rnFuns = ({
  linear: x =&gt; x,
  relu: x =&gt; Math.max(0, x),
  relu2: x =&gt; { const y = Math.max(0, x); return y * y; },
  clip: x =&gt; Math.min(1, Math.max(0, x)),
  sigmoid: x =&gt; 1 / (1 + Math.exp(-4 * (x - 1)))
})
